WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:04.100
-Beautiful day in New York City.
Temperatures are climbing.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.701
People are starting to
make summer plans,

00:00:05.701 --> 00:00:08.300
and it's easy to get distracted
from the job at hand.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:11.601
And for my writers, that job is
writing monologue jokes.

00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:14.667
You may not know this, but my
writers write hundreds of jokes

00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:16.234
every day just to get
the 10 or 12 we end up using

00:00:16.234 --> 00:00:17.601
in the monologue.

00:00:17.601 --> 00:00:21.234
And sometimes, to be honest,
I think they just throw in

00:00:21.234 --> 00:00:23.868
a few stinkers to fill the page

00:00:23.868 --> 00:00:25.234
and meet their daily assignment.

00:00:25.234 --> 00:00:28.067
But just because the warblers
are back in Central Park

00:00:28.067 --> 00:00:29.767
doesn't mean
we can tolerate laziness,

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:32.601
which means it's time for
another "Surprise Inspection."

00:00:32.601 --> 00:00:34.601
[ Fanfare ]

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.801
So, here is how
"Surprise Inspection" works.

00:00:41.801 --> 00:00:44.968
I'm going to read some real
jokes that were really submitted

00:00:44.968 --> 00:00:46.667
by my writers that are

00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:49.734
absolutely unusable
for this show.

00:00:50.234 --> 00:00:51.868
Some are dumb,
some are offensive,

00:00:51.868 --> 00:00:53.300
some are confusing.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.868
Most importantly, the writers
don't know we're doing this.

00:00:55.868 --> 00:00:58.367
And because a lot of them
don't watch the show,

00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:00.067
they'll never know.

00:01:01.501 --> 00:01:02.767
So, let's get started.

00:01:02.767 --> 00:01:04.868
Authorities in Philadelphia
recently picked up

00:01:04.868 --> 00:01:07.901
two goats that were spotted
walking through a neighborhood.

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:10.267
I'd tell you what the goats
said when they were arrested,

00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:12.968
but we'd have to bleat it out.

00:01:15.501 --> 00:01:18.367
A young man named Ian
wrote that joke.

00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:19.400
He looks like this.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.267
"Did you -- Did you get it?"

00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:36.701
President Biden
is set to meet tomorrow

00:01:36.701 --> 00:01:39.300
with the Italian prime minister,
where they will discuss

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.901
the concerning rise of anti-pa.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:52.734
So, that joke is
about antifa and antipasto.

00:01:57.968 --> 00:02:00.467
I forget -- who wrote it?
Can we take a look?

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:11.601
We're off and running.

00:02:11.601 --> 00:02:14.834
Researchers at MIT
published a study this week

00:02:14.834 --> 00:02:17.400
that details the best way
to split an Oreo.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.701
And this is pretty surprising.

00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:21.200
It's with your butt cheeks.

00:02:24.567 --> 00:02:28.200
Like, so, we --
Somebody we pay money to

00:02:29.701 --> 00:02:31.067
says, "I got a computer."

00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:35.901
And they're like, "Okay.
I'll show this to my boss."

00:02:37.968 --> 00:02:41.200
Let's take a look at Matt.
Matt wrote that. There you go.

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:46.400
Ooh.

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:50.701
Today was
Manatee Appreciation Day,

00:02:50.701 --> 00:02:52.868
so give 'em some props.

00:02:52.868 --> 00:02:54.868
[ Audience groans ]

00:02:58.234 --> 00:03:02.400
Kind of miss the butt cheeks
a little bit?

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:06.133
Kind of remembering the good old
days of the butt-cheek Oreos?

00:03:07.534 --> 00:03:08.601
Brian wrote that.

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:13.300
A couple in Massachusetts
was recently awarded

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.534
nearly $5 million in a lawsuit
against a local country club

00:03:16.534 --> 00:03:19.968
after enduring years of golf
balls crashing into their home.

00:03:19.968 --> 00:03:23.901
This after the club
only offered them "fore!"

00:03:24.968 --> 00:03:26.968
[ Breathes deeply ]

00:03:28.801 --> 00:03:30.801
So, that joke
required you to remember

00:03:30.801 --> 00:03:34.667
that they settled for $5 million

00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:37.367
and then the whole joke,
and then it got to "fore."

00:03:37.367 --> 00:03:39.200
And that was --
Hey, you know what?

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.234
That was Brian.

00:03:40.234 --> 00:03:42.934
Why don't we give him
some props.

00:03:42.934 --> 00:03:46.534
[ Applause ]
Yeah.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:51.367
Authorities in Florida
recently arrested a man

00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:53.567
who allegedly stole
a crossbow from a store

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:54.968
by stuffing it down his pants.

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:58.534
That or his penis
can shoot arrows.

00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:03.067
Karen!

00:04:08.934 --> 00:04:12.634
"That or his penis
can shoot arrows."

00:04:15.067 --> 00:04:18.234
I don't know if -- I don't think
it makes any sense.

00:04:22.634 --> 00:04:24.901
"You don't understand!"

00:04:25.467 --> 00:04:29.901
The worst is, that guy, the poor
man whose penis shoots arrows --

00:04:29.901 --> 00:04:31.834
his wife's like, "We stopped by
the sporting-goods store."

00:04:31.834 --> 00:04:36.067
He's like, "You know,
every time I go in there,

00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:38.400
they think I'm
stealing a crossbow."

00:04:39.934 --> 00:04:42.767
According to a new study,
having a long-term pet

00:04:42.767 --> 00:04:45.834
may delay memory loss,
though the brain-eating parasite

00:04:45.834 --> 00:04:48.934
in your cat's poop
may have other plans.

00:04:51.667 --> 00:04:53.367
That was from Ali. There's Ali.

00:04:53.367 --> 00:04:58.000
She seems very -- Now, if you've
been watching our show

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.901
for any amount of time,
first of all, ha!

00:05:00.901 --> 00:05:05.934
Second of all, you know that
the worst offender

00:05:05.934 --> 00:05:07.934
on our writing staff
when it comes to writing

00:05:07.934 --> 00:05:09.968
terrible, unusable, monstrous
jokes is our writer

00:05:09.968 --> 00:05:11.033
Mike Scollins.

00:05:11.033 --> 00:05:12.100
Let's take a look at Mike.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.167
There he is. Nope, that's Ali.

00:05:14.167 --> 00:05:16.667
And that's Mike right there.

00:05:16.667 --> 00:05:18.267
He once wore a tank top to work.

00:05:18.267 --> 00:05:25.000
So, the thing about Mike is,
he writes really good jokes.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.534
He's a very good joke writer.
But he has no middle ground.

00:05:28.534 --> 00:05:34.701
They are either monologue-worthy
or absolute shocking garbage.

00:05:34.701 --> 00:05:37.400
So let's just turn the spotlight
on Mike for a few jokes here.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:38.767
I'll get through them
as fast as I can

00:05:38.767 --> 00:05:40.133
so it won't hurt that much.

00:05:40.133 --> 00:05:42.501
Russian forces have reportedly
placed trained dolphins

00:05:42.501 --> 00:05:44.734
at the entrance to a port
on the Black Sea

00:05:44.734 --> 00:05:46.067
to protect a naval base.

00:05:46.067 --> 00:05:49.167
And they're still happier than
the ones at SeaWorld.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.434
A man from Illinois
has gained attention online

00:05:54.434 --> 00:05:57.133
after he claims to have found a
60-year-old McDonald's meal

00:05:57.133 --> 00:05:58.534
behind his bathroom wall.

00:05:58.534 --> 00:06:00.634
Also, Grimace.

00:06:02.367 --> 00:06:03.968
Pretty good.

00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:10.300
I mean, that's pretty good.
We could've done that one.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.367
You know what?

00:06:11.367 --> 00:06:12.834
I will say,
like, this is also --

00:06:12.834 --> 00:06:15.267
Like, there's a poor
graphics department that, like,

00:06:15.267 --> 00:06:16.634
Mike writes
these terrible things,

00:06:16.634 --> 00:06:20.400
and then they got to go --
Like, that's their job today

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.334
is like, "Well, we got to put

00:06:22.334 --> 00:06:25.200
the skeleton of Grimace
in a wall."

00:06:26.701 --> 00:06:29.534
Netflix announced yesterday
it will develop five new shows

00:06:29.534 --> 00:06:32.501
for preschoolers based on
famous Dr. Seuss books.

00:06:32.501 --> 00:06:35.367
But you're not going to like
who's directing "Hop on Pop."

00:06:35.367 --> 00:06:37.000
Guys...
[ Audience groans ]

00:06:46.701 --> 00:06:49.400
He's right --
they weren't going to like it.

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:53.033
The Tony Award nominees
were announced yesterday,

00:06:53.033 --> 00:06:55.534
and the musical "MJ" about the
late singer Michael Jackson --

00:06:55.534 --> 00:06:58.400
So, guys, let's just
prepare ourselves.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.868
Okay?

00:07:00.868 --> 00:07:03.033
The Tony award nominees
were announced yesterday,

00:07:03.033 --> 00:07:05.801
and the musical "MJ" about the
late singer Michael Jackson --

00:07:05.801 --> 00:07:07.334
Because I think we should be
able to jump ahead

00:07:07.334 --> 00:07:08.734
and be like, "Which part of
Michael Jackson?

00:07:08.734 --> 00:07:14.133
What's the joke?"

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:16.167
The Tony Award nominees
were announced yesterday,

00:07:16.167 --> 00:07:18.667
and the musical "MJ" about the
late singer Michael Jackson

00:07:18.667 --> 00:07:19.968
received 10 nominations.

00:07:19.968 --> 00:07:23.434
Well, it is supposed
to be touching.

00:07:30.801 --> 00:07:32.200
Mike Scollins, beat it.

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:34.467
This has been
"Surprise Inspection."

00:07:34.467 --> 00:07:36.467
[ Fanfare ]

